usage: flair transcriptome [-h] -b GENOME_ALIGNED_BAM -g GENOME --sample_name
                           SAMPLE_NAME [-o OUTPUT] [-f ANNOT_GTF]
                           [--junction_tab JUNCTION_TAB]
                           [--junction_bed JUNCTION_BED]
                           [--junction_support JUNCTION_SUPPORT]
                           [--ss_window SS_WINDOW] [--end_window END_WINDOW]
                           [--sjc_support SJC_SUPPORT]
                           [--single_exon_support SINGLE_EXON_SUPPORT]
                           [--frac_support FRAC_SUPPORT] [--trust_strand]
                           [--trust_ends] [--no_stringent] [--no_check_splice]
                           [--no_align_to_annot] [--max_ends MAX_ENDS]
                           [--filter {nosubset,bysupport,comprehensive,ginormous}]
                           [--keep_supplementary] [--quality QUALITY]
                           [--allow_paralogs] [-t THREADS]
                           [--parallel_mode PARALLEL_MODE]
                           [--fusion_breakpoints FUSION_BREAKPOINTS]
                           [--keep_intermediate] [--normalize_ends]
                           [--generate_map]

generates confident transcript models directly from a bam file of aligned long
rna-seq reads

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  -o OUTPUT, --output OUTPUT
                        output file name base for FLAIR isoforms, defaults to
                        --sample_name
  -f ANNOT_GTF, --gtf ANNOT_GTF
                        GTF annotation file, used for identifying annotated
                        isoforms
  --junction_tab JUNCTION_TAB
                        short-read junctions in SJ.out.tab format. Use this
                        option if you aligned your short-reads with STAR, STAR
                        will automatically output this file
  --junction_bed JUNCTION_BED
                        short-read junctions in bed format (can be generated
                        from long-read alignment with intron-prospector)
  --junction_support JUNCTION_SUPPORT
                        if providing short-read junctions, minimum junction
                        support required to keep junction. If your junctions
                        file is in bed format, the score field will be used
                        for read support (default: 2)
  --ss_window SS_WINDOW
                        window size for correcting splice sites (default: 15)
  --end_window END_WINDOW
                        window size for comparing TSS/TES (default: 100)
  --sjc_support SJC_SUPPORT
                        minimum number of supporting reads for a spliced
                        isoform (default: 1)
  --single_exon_support SINGLE_EXON_SUPPORT
                        minimum number of supporting reads for a single exon
                        isoform (default: 3)
  --frac_support FRAC_SUPPORT
                        minimum fraction of gene locus support for isoform to
                        be called; only isoforms that make up more than this
                        fraction of the gene locus are reported. Set to 0 for
                        max recall (default: 0.05)
  --trust_strand        trust the stranding of the input reads and do not
                        attempt strand correction
  --trust_ends          trust the ends of the input reads: a more stringent
                        way of requiring read ends to match the ends of
                        transcript models
  --no_stringent        do not require all supporting reads to be full-length,
                        that is aligned to the first and last exons of the
                        transcript. Use this for fragmented libraries, with an
                        understanding that it will impact precision
  --no_check_splice     do not enforce accurate alignment around splice sites.
                        Specify this for libraries with high error rates, but
                        it will reduce precision
  --no_align_to_annot   skip the initial alignment to the annotated sequences
                        and detect transcripts only from the genomic
                        alignment. Slightly faster but less accurate when the
                        annotation is good
  --max_ends MAX_ENDS   maximum number of TSS/TES picked per isoform; make
                        higher for more precise end detection (default: 1)
  --filter {nosubset,bysupport,comprehensive,ginormous}
                        nosubset: any isoforms that are a proper set of
                        another isoform are removed; bysupport: subset
                        isoforms are removed based on support; comprehensive:
                        default set plus all subset isoforms; ginormous:
                        comprehensive set plus single exon subset isoforms
                        (default: nosubset)
  --keep_supplementary  keep supplementary alignments when defining isoforms
  --quality QUALITY     minimum mapping quality threshold to consider genomic
                        alignments for defining transcripts (default: 1)
  --allow_paralogs      NOT IMPLEMENTED: assign reads to multiple paralogs
                        with equivalent alignment. Specifying this is an error
                        rather than a no-op
  -t THREADS, --threads THREADS
                        number of threads to run with, related to
                        --parallel_mode (default: 12)
  --parallel_mode PARALLEL_MODE
                        parallelization mode. auto:1GB is an automatic
                        threshold where, if the file is less than 1GB,
                        parallelization is done by chromosome, and if it is
                        larger, parallelization is done by region of non-
                        overlapping reads. Other modes: bychrom, byregion,
                        auto:xGB; for the auto threshold, the size must be in
                        units of GB (default: auto:1GB)
  --fusion_breakpoints FUSION_BREAKPOINTS
                        for fusion detection only: bed file containing
                        locations of fusion breakpoints on the synthetic
                        genome
  --keep_intermediate   keep intermediate and temporary files for debugging.
                        Intermediate files include the promoter-supported
                        reads file and read assignments to firstpass isoforms
  --normalize_ends      normalize transcript ends with similar terminal splice
                        sites; only recommended when --max_ends is 1
  --generate_map        generate a txt file of read-isoform assignments

required named arguments:
  -b GENOME_ALIGNED_BAM, --genome_aligned_bam GENOME_ALIGNED_BAM
                        Sorted and indexed bam file aligned to the genome
  -g GENOME, --genome GENOME
                        FastA of reference genome, can be minimap2 indexed
  --sample_name SAMPLE_NAME
                        name of sample - this will be added as metadata to
                        your output files
